38
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:29.690
Well, Turf War was written by Warren Lieberstein and Halstead Sullivan and directed by Danny Chun.
《地盘之争》由沃伦·利伯斯坦和霍尔斯特德·沙利文编剧，丹尼·春执导。

39
00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.410
Here's your summary.
这是剧情梗概。

40
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:39.310
In this episode, we find out that Robert California got really drunk the night before and decided to close the Binghamton branch.
在这一集中，我们发现罗伯特·加利福尼亚前一天晚上喝得酩酊大醉，决定关闭宾汉姆顿分公司。

41
00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:45.110
Jim and Dwight start poaching their clients, which angers a salesman from the Syracuse branch.
吉姆和德怀特开始抢夺他们的客户，这激怒了雪城分公司的一名销售员。

42
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:51.150
And the three fight for Syracuse's biggest client, Prestige Direct Mail Solutions.
于是三人为了争夺雪城最大的客户“尊贵直邮解决方案公司”而展开了较量。

43
00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:59.750
This also sparks an idea in Andy, who is still hanging around the Dunder Mifflin offices despite no longer working there.
这也让安迪产生了一个想法，尽管他已经不在那里工作了，却依然在邓德·米夫林办公室周围晃荡。

44
00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:08.310
And finally, Robert asks Pam and Angela to find out what was in his drunken voicemail to Nellie from the night before.
最后，罗伯特让帕姆和安吉拉去查清楚他前一天晚上喝醉后给奈莉留的语音留言里说了什么。

45
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.430
I loved that storyline.
我喜欢那条故事线。

46
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:13.490
I loved girl talk in the break room.
我喜欢休息室里的闺蜜谈话。

47
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.090
I loved how Robert brought Angela in.
我喜欢罗伯特把安吉拉也拉进来的方式。

48
00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.110
As a backup.
作为后备。

49
00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.270
Because he just did not trust Pam to get this job done.
因为他就是不相信帕姆能搞定这件事。

50
00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:24.190
I have a little tidbit, lady.
我有个小花絮要分享，女士。

51
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.510
What's your tidbit?
你有什么趣闻吗？

52
00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:32.050
This episode's original air date was May 3rd, 2012, which happened to be my daughter's fourth birthday.
这一集的首播日期是2012年5月3日，正好是我女儿的四岁生日。

53
00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:33.790
Oh!
噢！

54
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:34.730
I know.
我知道。

55
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:36.430
A happy birthday episode.
真是个生日快乐集。

56
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:37.330
Yeah.
是啊。

57
00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.910
And of course, it made me go back in my digital clutter of all these adorable photos of her when she was four.
当然，这让我回去翻看了我电脑里乱七八糟存着的她四岁时那些可爱的照片。

58
00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:42.910
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

59
00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.450
That's a cute age.
那个年纪真可爱。

60
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:45.570
Oh, cute.
噢，太可爱了。

61
00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.990
Are you ready for Fast Fact No.
准备好听第一个快讯了吗？

62
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:48.330
1?
1？

63
00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:49.350
So ready.
准备好了。

64
00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.130
Our writer, Danny Chun, directed this episode.
我们的编剧丹尼·春执导了这一集。

65
00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.950
It was his directing debut.
这是他的导演处女作。

66
00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.350
And we reached out to Danny about this episode.
我们联系了丹尼聊了聊这一集。

67
00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.370
He sent in some really fun tidbits for us to share.
他发来了一些非常有趣的趣闻供我们分享。

68
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:02.750
He sure did, Danny.
他确实发了，丹尼。

69
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:03.790
Thank you so much.
非常感谢你。

70
00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:08.090
We started by asking him what it was like to move from writer to director.
我们首先问他从编剧转行做导演是什么感觉。

71
00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:13.890
And he said it was so much fun, but it really misled him for future directing jobs
他说这非常有趣，但这也让他对未来的导演工作产生了误解，

72
00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:19.750
because every other directing experience he's had since The Office has been way harder and way less fun.
因为自《办公室》之后，他所有的导演经历都比这次要困难得多，也无趣得多。

73
00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:20.470
Oh.
噢。

74
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:21.130
Sorry, Danny.
抱歉了，丹尼。

75
00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:28.730
He also said that The Office cast was so good, he never really had to stress out about performances,
他还说《办公室》的演员阵容太棒了，他从来不需要为表演感到压力，

76
00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.890
and the crew could basically shoot an episode in their sleep.
而且剧组基本上闭着眼睛都能拍完一集。

77
00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:36.330
But Danny really wanted to give a special shout-out to our script supervisor, Veda,
但丹尼特别想感谢我们的场记维达，

78
00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:44.050
who he said saved him from embarrassment about, quote, 500 times by reminding him what shots we still needed.
他说维达提醒了他大概500次还需要补拍哪些镜头，让他免于尴尬。

79
00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:47.070
He said she was so meticulous, and thank God we had her.
他说她非常细致，谢天谢地我们有她。

80
00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:48.850
Veda was the beSt.
维达是最棒的。

81
00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.050
She really was the beSt.
她确实是最棒的。

82
00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:56.770
So in addition to tracking all the continuity, she also kept track of the director's shot list
所以除了追踪所有的连续性，她还负责跟进导演的拍摄清单，

83
00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:02.510
and made sure that the director got every angle and everything that he would need to turn over to the editor.
并确保导演拍到了所有需要的角度和素材，以便交给剪辑师。

84
00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.770
She was a tracker, a major tracker.
她是个追踪者，超级追踪者。

85
00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.810
She was a major tracker and hyper-focused.
她是个超级追踪者，而且极其专注。

86
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.710
Like, her little podium, I mean, she was so focused.
就像她的小讲台，我是说，她真的太专注了。

87
00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:13.650
I don't think there was anyone as focused all day as Veda.
我觉得整天下来没人能像维达那样专注。

88
00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:14.530
Yeah.
是啊。

89
00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:22.790
Danny also shared that in terms of directing, he was most intimidated whenever he had to give James Spader a note.
丹尼还分享说，在导演方面，他最害怕的就是给詹姆斯·斯派德提意见。

90
00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.730
Oh, I would be too.
噢，我也会的。

91
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:25.730
Forget it.
算了吧。

92
00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:26.610
I know.
我知道。

93
00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:31.950
He said that he would give him a little note, like an example would be,
他说他会给詹姆斯提个小建议，比如举个例子，

94
00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:35.630
hey, why don't you try to play this next one with a little more confusion?
嘿，你能不能试着把下一场戏演得更困惑一点？

95
00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:40.630
And that James would just pause and stare at him and say, why?
然后詹姆斯就会停下来盯着他问：为什么？

96
00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.010
Oh, no.
噢，不。

97
00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:45.010
And Danny said, quote, it was so withering.
丹尼说，引用他的话，那感觉真是让人如坐针毡。

98
00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:51.150
He said he would try to scramble and kind of articulate his reasoning in some long-winded way,
他说他会试图手忙脚乱地用长篇大论来阐述自己的理由，

99
00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.810
even though the real answer was just because it's funnier.
尽管真正的答案只是因为那样更好笑。

100
00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:58.950
And he said James Spader would just stare at him and he wouldn't give any reaction,
他说詹姆斯·斯派德只会盯着他看，没有任何反应。

101
00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.770
but then he would always take the direction and it would be great because he's such an incredible actor.
但后来他总是会接受指导，效果也总是很棒，因为他是一位如此出色的演员。

102
00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:09.330
I imagine it's got to be really intimidating to give anyone a note.
我想给任何人提意见一定都非常令人胆怯。

103
00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.110
You know, it's just got to be, ah, ah.
你知道，那一定得是，啊，啊。

104
00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:18.390
Well, in his description of giving James Spader a note reminded me of every theater actor getting a note.
嗯，他描述给詹姆斯·斯派德提意见的过程，让我想起了每个戏剧演员收到意见时的样子。

105
00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.290
Because theater actors, we need to know why.
因为作为戏剧演员，我们需要知道原因。

106
00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:23.190
What are you thinking?
你在想什么？

107
00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.350
What's my motivation?
我的动机是什么？

108
00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.710
I can't just be confused.
我不能只是感到困惑。

109
00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.690
I have to know why I am confused.
我必须知道我为什么困惑。

110
00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.210
What has confused me?
是什么让我困惑？

111
00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.110
How long have I been confused for?
我困惑多久了？

112
00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:34.770
Am I a person that gets confused easily?
我是那种容易困惑的人吗？

113
00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:36.090
Yes, exactly.
是的，没错。

114
00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:37.170
You got it, Anne.
你说对了，安妮。

115
00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:43.510
Where is, like, I feel like I have both brains because I can overanalyze a note,
怎么说呢，我觉得我拥有两种大脑，因为我既能过度分析一个意见，

116
00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:48.010
but I can also understand I don't need to know why.
也能理解我其实不需要知道为什么。

117
00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.170
It's just funnier if I act confused.
如果我直接表现出困惑，反而更好笑。

118
00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:51.430
So I'll just do it.
所以我就会直接照做。

119
00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:58.030
That, I feel like, is a real, like, comedic sketch comics approach to a note like that.
我觉得这是一种非常喜剧小品演员式的处理意见的方法。

120
00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:58.610
Got it.
明白了。

121
00:05:58.750 --> 00:05:59.110
Funnier.
更好笑。

122
00:05:59.270 --> 00:05:59.630
Do it.
照做。

123
00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:00.290
Yeah.
是的。

124
00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:01.090
Next.
下一个。

125
00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:01.670
Move on.
继续。

126
00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:02.350
Yeah.
是的。

127
00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:02.890
Yeah.
是的。

128
00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.850
Are you ready for Fast Fact No.
准备好迎接第二个快问快答了吗？

129
00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:05.170
2?
2？

130
00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:06.310
Yes.
准备好了。

131
00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:07.970
Fast Fact No.
快问快答第

132
00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:08.390
2.
2条。

